<slide>
  justify: true
  halign: start
  valign: start
</slide>

<h2>Alignment: everything starts at the top left</h2>

<p>
  Every box starts at the <b>start</b>, both ways: text at the left edge, at the top. Say otherwise with
  <c>halign</c> and <c>valign</c>, each <c>start</c>, <c>center</c> or <c>end</c>. They work in the <c><slide></c> block of a slide, on a row, a col or a text tag,
  in <c>defaults</c> in <c>deck.json</c>, and on a <c><code></c> (<c>halign=center</c>, <c>valign=end</c>).
</p>

<p>
  <c>justify: true</c> stretches every line of a paragraph to the full column width, like in a printed document; the last line stays at the start.
  This slide uses it. <c>defaults</c> in <c>deck.json</c> can switch it on for a whole deck.
</p>

<p>
  <c>deck.json</c> can set defaults for the two shapes of slide: <c>columns</c> for slides with two columns (text next to code), for
  example <c>{"valign": "center"}</c>, and <c>textOnly</c> for slides without code, for example <c>{"halign": "center", "valign": "center"}</c>.
  This deck centers its text-only slides; this one opts out with <c>halign: start</c> and <c>valign: start</c>.
</p>

<notes>
  start/center/end are also accepted as left/right/top/middle/bottom.
</notes>
